Third-party software distributed in the container image
-------------------------------------------------------

The lftpweb container image bundles unmodified third-party programs from the Alpine
Linux package repositories. They are aggregated with lftpweb, not derived from it or
linked into it — lftpweb invokes them as separate processes — so each remains under
its own licence:

  lftp             GPL-3.0-or-later   https://lftp.yar.ru/
  openssh-client   BSD-style          https://www.openssh.com/
  7zip (7zz)       LGPL-2.1-or-later, with the unRAR restriction applying to its
                   RAR decoding code   https://www.7-zip.org/
  su-exec          MIT                https://github.com/ncopa/su-exec
  tini             MIT                https://github.com/krallin/tini

Complete corresponding source for these packages is available from the Alpine Linux
project at https://pkgs.alpinelinux.org/ — the image ships them unmodified, exactly as
packaged there.

The image also bundles a program built from third-party source, rather than an
unmodified Alpine package — the distinction matters for where its complete
corresponding source lives:

  unrar            unRAR licence (freeware; source-derived   https://www.rarlab.com/
                   restriction below)

`unrar` is compiled by `docker/Dockerfile`'s `unrar-builder` stage directly from
RARLAB's published unrarsrc release (currently 7.2.3), unmodified apart from the build
flags recorded in that Dockerfile stage; the source itself is not vendored into this
repository. It is aggregated with lftpweb the same way the Alpine packages above are —
invoked as a separate process, not linked into lftpweb — so it remains under its own
licence. Complete corresponding source is available directly from RARLAB at
https://www.rarlab.com/rar_add.htm.

Note on lftp: lftpweb runs lftp as a subprocess and communicates with it at arm's
length. It does not link against lftp, and is not a derivative work of it.

Note on 7zip's RAR support: 7-Zip's RAR decoder derives from the unRAR source, whose
licence forbids using it to develop a RAR-compatible archiver. lftpweb only extracts
archives; it never creates them. Alpine's own `7zip` package ships without any RAR
codec at all (verified against the built image, not just read about) — this is why
`unrar` above exists in this image separately from `7zz`.

Note on unrar: the UnRAR source licence is freeware. It permits using and
redistributing the UnRAR utility, including inside other software packages, without
limitation and free of charge, provided this notice accompanies it. It forbids only
using the UnRAR source to develop a RAR (WinRAR) compatible *archiver* or to
re-implement RAR's proprietary compression algorithm. lftpweb only extracts archives
with it; it never creates or re-compresses one, so this restriction does not apply to
how lftpweb uses it. Full licence text: https://www.rarlab.com/license.htm
